AddDbContextModelSnapShot.cs under Migration folder keeps current database , tables and columns info. Any new migration update will generate a 
migration file and update this snapshot file with latest schema.

A new migration file used to get created under migration folder as and when any migration command is ran. File name would look like 
290920201230<timestamp>_migrationName.cs. This file contains 2 methods Up() and Down(). Up() method keeps track of current changes in migration. 
Down() method contains info that needs to be removed when we wish to downgrade any migration update.

Suppose we have added column1 in a migration, then Migration file will look like as below.
public partial class MigrationName : Migration {
	protected override void Up(MigrationBuilder migrationBuilder){
		migrationBuilder.AddColumn<string>(name: "Column1", table:"EmployeeTable", nullable: true);
	}
	protected override void Down(MigrationBuilder migrationBuilder) {
		migrationBuilder.DropColumn(name: "Column1", table:"EmployeeTable", nullable: true);
	}
}

In order to remove a migration we should use "remove-migration" command in package manager console. It will revert last recent migration. 
This will remove last migration file and will modify AddDbContextModelSnapShot.cs content so that it can point to last but one migration file.

"Remove migration" command will work if and only if changes are not applied on database. Once applied on database, this command will not work
and will give error. 

"update-database" command will update all pending migration commands on database.

By default EF creates a table in database named "__EFMigrationHistory". This table keeps track of database migration history. In order to 
revert a database migration , we need to run "update-database migration3" and our database structure will be reverted back to migration3 update.
Then we have to run "remove migration" to delete unwanted migration file from migration folder.

