**************** Autherization in .net core ***********************************
Authentication is the process of identifying who the user is. 
Autherization is the process of identifying what the user can and can't do. 

We can achieve Autherization by decorating Autherize attibure over any action. If any un-Autherized user tries to access 
any Autherized action, application will redirect the user to Login page and here url will look like
	baseUrl/Account/Login?ReturnUrl=home/edit/1 (assuming user was redirected to login page after home/edit/1 URL)
Once user logsin, app can redirect him back to ReturnUrl by adding few extra code.

using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication;
[Autherize]
public IActionResult Create(){
}

When we apply Autherize attribute on Controller, non of the controller action is accessible for a non-autherized user. 
In this case , if we wish to allow un-Autherized user to index page, we should decorate it with AllowAnonymous attribute.

[Autherize]
public class HomeController{

	[AllowAnonymous]
	public IActionResult Index(){
	}

	public IActionResult EditUser(){
	}
	
	public IActionResult Create(){
	}
}

But if we apply AllowAnonymous attribute over controller and Autherize attribute over any action, that action can be accessed by
un-autherized user.

We can add Autherize attribute globally in startup.cs too. Hence all controller action methods are accessible to autherized user.
Startup.cs

using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Autherization;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Autherization;
public void ConfigureSerives(IServiceCollection services){
	services.addMvc(options=>{
		var policy = new AuthenticationPolicyBuilder()
						.RequiredAuthenticatedUser()
						.Build();
		options.Filters.Add(new AutherizeFilter(policy));
	});
}

